<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Man of 51 with a new 1.5 cm firm, painless lump eccentric to the left nipple and a palpable left axillary node; breast cancer suspected. Diagnostic mammography, ultrasound, and core biopsy are booked for tomorrow. Please see him as soon as the results are back to plan surgery. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent suspected-cancer pathway, within one week. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>If the biopsy confirms cancer, what is the clinical stage, and does he need staging scans before surgery?</item><item>What operation do you recommend for the breast, and how should the axilla be managed if the node is positive?</item><item>Should germline testing be done before surgery, and would its result change the operation?</item><item>What should he expect after surgery, including drains, shoulder movement, and lymphedema prevention?</item><item>Who will plan treatment after surgery, and when?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Roundtree is seen at the request of Dr. Carrington. A few days before his first visit he found a painless lump under the left nipple in the shower and thought at first that it was the breast enlargement he had as a boy. Diagnostic mammography and ultrasound of both breasts and both axillae five days ago showed a 1.6 cm irregular retroareolar mass on the left and one abnormal left axillary node, with a normal right breast and axilla. Core biopsies of both show invasive ductal carcinoma, grade 3, ER 95 percent, PR 85 percent, HER2 1+, Ki-67 30 percent, with metastatic carcinoma in the node. He has no bone pain, cough, headache, or weight loss. He is fit, trains most days, and works as an actor. He says he did not know a man could get breast cancer, and he is worried about privacy at work. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Richard Roundtree was born on July 9, 1942, in New Rochelle, New York. [D]</item><item>Roundtree was an actor best known as the private detective John Shaft in films beginning in the early 1970s, and is described as the first Black action hero. [D]</item><item>Roundtree told PEOPLE that at the end of September 1993, in the shower, he felt a lump under his left nipple, &apos;the size of a lima bean&apos;; he was not overly concerned at first, because at 13 he had had gynecomastia (breast enlargement), and he &apos;just chalked it up to that.&apos; [D]</item><item>In a 2019 television interview Roundtree said he found the lump in the shower while working in Costa Rica in 1993 and saw his doctor after returning to Los Angeles a week later; the doctor biopsied it and four or five days later told him he had breast cancer. Asked whether he had felt shame, he answered &apos;Embarrassment,&apos; and he said men &apos;are so cavalier about their health issues and want to sweep it under the rug.&apos; [D]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

