<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Invasive ductal carcinoma of the left breast, clinical stage IIA (cT1c cN1 M0), ER and PR positive, HER2 negative, grade 3 (C50.122). Men get breast cancer. In men it is usually retroareolar, as here, and usually hormone receptor-positive. He has early, operable disease with a biopsy-proven axillary node. Staging scans are not recommended for clinical stage IIA without symptoms or abnormal blood tests. Because the tumor is small and hormone receptor-positive, surgery first is reasonable; chemotherapy before surgery was discussed as an alternative, and surgery first gives the full pathology to guide treatment afterwards. With so little breast tissue in men, mastectomy is the usual operation, and with a biopsy-proven node, a level I and II axillary dissection is standard. [S]</item><item>Germline testing. Germline testing should be offered to every man with breast cancer. BRCA2 is the gene most often found. A variant would raise the risk of a new cancer in the other breast and of prostate and pancreatic cancer, and would matter for his relatives. It need not delay surgery, because the operation on the cancer side is the same either way. [S]</item><item>Concern about privacy and stigma. He feels embarrassed by a breast cancer diagnosis and fears it could cost him work. These are common reasons men delay care or keep it secret. He came in promptly, which is the right thing, and his care can be kept confidential. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Diagnostic mammography and ultrasound of both breasts and both axillae; core biopsy pathology of the breast mass and the axillary node with ER, PR, HER2, and Ki-67; today&apos;s preoperative blood count and chemistry, all normal. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Left total mastectomy with level I and II axillary lymph node dissection in two days. [S]</item><item>Removal of the other breast is not needed for cancer control; some men choose it for symmetry, and that choice is his. It does not need to wait for the germline result. Reconstruction is possible but rarely chosen by men; offered. [S]</item><item>No staging CT, bone scan, or PET before surgery. [S]</item><item>Genetic counseling and a germline multigene panel (BRCA2, BRCA1, PALB2, CHEK2, ATM, and others), sent today. [S]</item><item>Measure both upper arms before surgery as the lymphedema baseline. [S]</item><item>After surgery: a drain for about a week, shoulder exercises from the first day, numbness under the arm is expected, and lymphedema risk reduction for the left arm. [S]</item><item>Medical oncology after the pathology, to plan chemotherapy and endocrine therapy; tamoxifen is the first-choice endocrine therapy for men. Radiation oncology review if two or more nodes, or other high-risk features, are found. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Carrington today. The patient agrees with the plan and asked for his records to be kept confidential; written information about breast cancer in men was given. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Operation in two days. Surgical review of the wound and pathology in about ten days. Medical oncology after the pathology. Primary care continues distress screening and preventive care before treatment. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
